15.4  syscalls and strace

TL;DR
  - Programs don't touch files, the network or devices themselves: they ask the
    kernel, with system calls (openat, read, connect…).
  - strace shows every system call a program makes, with its arguments and
    result. Errors such as ENOENT (no such file) stand out.
  - -f follows child processes, -e filters, -p attaches to a running process.

      strace -f -e trace=openat ./myapp 2>&1 | grep ENOENT
      strace -c ls               # count calls, summary at the end
      sudo strace -p 1234

On your machine
  vm.swappiness                    60  how willing the kernel is to swap
  vm.overcommit_memory              0  0 guess, 1 always say yes, 2 strict limit
  kernel.dmesg_restrict             1  1: reading dmesg needs root
  net.ipv4.ip_forward               1  1: this machine routes packets
  kernel.sysrq                    176  0 off, 1 all, other: some Alt+SysRq keys
  fs.inotify.max_user_watches  524288  files a user can watch for changes
  Set in    vm.swappiness = 10 (/etc/sysctl.d/90-swap.conf)
            fs.inotify.max_user_watches = 524288 (/etc/sysctl.d/99-inotify.conf)

Pitfalls
  - strace slows the traced program down a lot: don't leave it attached to a
    busy production service.
  - strace -p on your own process may fail: with kernel.yama.ptrace_scope = 1
    (Ubuntu's default), you may only trace your own child processes. Use sudo.
  - strace writes to stderr: add 2>&1 before a pipe, or -o trace.txt to write a
    file.
  - A failing call isn't always a problem: programs often try several paths and
    use the first that exists.

Deep dive

  ▾ What is a system call?

    The way a program asks the kernel for something it can't do alone: open a
    file, send a packet, start a process. The program puts the call number and
    arguments in CPU registers, and a special instruction switches to the
    kernel, which does the work and returns a result. Library functions such as
    fopen or printf end up calling them.

  ▾ How does strace work?

    Through ptrace, the same mechanism debuggers use: the kernel stops the
    traced program at every system call and lets strace read its registers.
    That's why it's slow. It's also why Yama restricts it: a tool that can trace
    your programs can read their memory, passwords included.

Related
  Commands  strace
  See also  idk 10.2 Signals
